abstract: "esMorph is a morphological analyzer of Spanish with a Rust core and a Python API. For a word form it finds the analyses in a dictionary built from the English and the Spanish Wiktionary - the lemma, the part of speech and the Universal Dependencies features following the UD guidelines and, where they are silent, the AnCora conventions - and orders them by probability by the counts of the AnCora and GSD treebanks or, in a sentence, by a small context model trained on these treebanks. Verbs with enclitic pronouns are analyzed with the words of the token, colloquial and old spellings as their standard words, and own words of a user as a sample word of the dictionary; punctuation, numbers, ordinals, Roman numerals, names, addresses and abbreviations are analyzed by rules, and unknown words are guessed by their endings, hyphenated parts and prefixes. Every analysis gives the forms of its lexeme and the form with the requested features: conjugation, gender and number. It annotates documents of the anySeg segmenter in context, and a command-line tool and a spaCy component analyze text in context."
